## Vercel Speed Insights Configuration

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for the Next.js project.

### Changes Made:

1. **Installed @vercel/speed-insights package** (v1.3.1)
   - Used npm to install the package as the project uses package-lock.json
   - Added to dependencies in package.json

2. **Modified app/layout.tsx**
   - Added import: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next"`
   - Placed `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<body>` tag after `{children}`
   - This follows the recommended pattern for Next.js 13.5+ with App Router

### Implementation Details:

The project uses:
- Next.js 16.1.6 (which is >= 13.5)
- App Router architecture
- npm as the package manager

Since the project is using Next.js 16.1.6 with App Router, I used the recommended approach:
- Imported from `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Added the component directly to the root layout without needing the `usePathname()` hook or route prop
- Placed the component at the end of the body tag to ensure it doesn't interfere with the main content rendering

### Verification:

✅ Build completed successfully with `npm run build`
✅ All routes compiled and generated correctly
✅ Linter checked (pre-existing linting issues in other files, none related to this change)
✅ Lock file (package-lock.json) properly updated

### Files Modified:

- `app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

The Speed Insights component will now automatically track and report Web Vitals metrics (CLS, FID, FCP, LCP, TTFB) when the application is deployed to Vercel.
